==English==

===Etymology===
The slang term jiggy has been around at least since the 1930s, and originally it meant 'crazy'. 

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{obsolete}} Being [[crazy]].
#:''He's gone completely '''jiggy'''.''
# Being [[jittery]], [[fidgety]], [[restless]], [[excited]].
#*'''1989.''' Radford & Crowley, ''Drug Agent'':
#*:''If I was too '''jiggy''' to hold the syringe, he'd shoot me up''. 
# Being [[wonderful]], [[excellent]], [[enjoyable]], [[exciting]], [[stylish]], [[cool]], [[successful]].
#*''Get yourself fsome ''jiggy'' gear.''
# Having [[fun]], enjoying oneself totally; losing one's inhibitions, especially when dancing or performing to music.
#*'''1997-1998.''' Will Smith, ''Get '''Jiggy''' With It.'' (song)
#*:''Just can't sit''
#*:''Gotta get '''jiggy''' wit' it''
#*'''1998.''' ''L.A. Times'':
#*:''Latin groovers get '''jiggy''' at the mercury-hot Conga Room on Wilshire Boulevard.''
#*'''1998.''' ''People Magazine'':
#*:''When Ally McBeal's writers decided to have ...Calista Flockhart get '''jiggy''' with an imaginary dancing baby..."

